<div class="print-page" style="--pc: #06b6d4">
  <header class="print-header">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
      <a href="/gcsl">GCSL</a>
    </nav>
    <h1>The Language</h1>
    <p class="print-note">Single-page view Â· <a href="/gcsl/language/print.md">as markdown</a></p>
  </header>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="values-and-bindings">Values and Bindings</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>GCSL programs compute values. A block can return one value or construct a
record of named fields, so you can use configuration data inside ordinary
expressions without a separate data format.</p>
<h2 id="values-and-bindings--blocks-and-records">Blocks and records <a class="heading-anchor" href="#values-and-bindings--blocks-and-records" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Newlines or semicolons separate items inside a file or <code>{ ... }</code> block.
A block without fields returns its final expression, or an explicit
<code>= expression</code> result. A block with fields returns their record instead;
it cannot also have an explicit result. The empty record <code>{}</code> represents
an operation with no useful result.</p>
<p>A field is a public part of the resulting record. A <code>let</code> binding is local
to its scope. This block keeps the intermediate name private:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let release = "0.1.0"
name = "gcsl"
archive = "{name}-{release}.tar.gz"
</code></pre></div>
<p>Top-level fields and named functions can refer to declarations written
later. Inner blocks evaluate their ordinary fields in order. Private
<code>let</code> bindings become available after their declaration.</p>
<h2 id="values-and-bindings--updating-a-binding">Updating a binding <a class="heading-anchor" href="#values-and-bindings--updating-a-binding" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>:=</code> replaces the value associated with a local name. For an ordinary
<code>let</code>, this is restricted to the block that declares it. Use <code>let mut</code>
when an inner block of the same function needs to update the variable:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let mut total = 0
for cost in [3, 8, 5] {
    total := total + cost
}
total
</code></pre></div>
<p>The variable keeps its declared or inferred type. Values themselves do not
change: replacing a record field or list element constructs an updated
value for the binding. Other bindings and previously created closures
keep the values they already held.</p>
<p>A closure captures values when it is created. It cannot update a mutable
variable belonging to its enclosing function. Pass in a value and return
an updated value when you need that communication.</p>
<h2 id="values-and-bindings--names-and-arithmetic">Names and arithmetic <a class="heading-anchor" href="#values-and-bindings--names-and-arithmetic" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Names can contain hyphens. Write spaces around subtraction so that
<code>left - right</code> is an operation rather than a name. Integer arithmetic uses
signed 64-bit values with checked overflow. Floating-point values use
64-bit precision. <code>/</code> produces a floating-point result; <code>//</code> and <code>%</code> are
integer floor division and modulo.</p>
<p>Integer literals adapt to an expected numeric type. A bound <code>Int</code> does
not silently become a <code>Float</code>; use <code>float(value)</code> when that conversion is
intended. Conditions require <code>Bool</code>, not a nonzero integer or a nonempty
string. See <code>gcsl(5)</code> for syntax and operator rules.</p>
<h2 id="values-and-bindings--durations-and-sizes">Durations and sizes <a class="heading-anchor" href="#values-and-bindings--durations-and-sizes" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>Duration</code> and <code>Size</code> hold amounts with units. Write a duration as a
number followed by units from largest to smallest: <code>30s</code>, <code>500ms</code>,
<code>1h30m</code>, <code>2d</code>. The units are <code>d</code>, <code>h</code>, <code>m</code>, <code>s</code>, <code>ms</code>, <code>us</code> and <code>ns</code>, and a
day is exactly 24 hours. Write a size with one unit: <code>512B</code>, the decimal
<code>KB</code> to <code>PB</code>, or the binary <code>KiB</code> to <code>PiB</code>, as in <code>2GiB</code>. The last part of
a literal can have a fraction if the result is a whole number of
nanoseconds or bytes, so <code>1.5s</code> and <code>0.5KiB</code> are allowed but <code>1.5ns</code> is
not. Units are case-sensitive.</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let timeout = 1m30s
retry_after = timeout // 3
budget = 2 * timeout
memory = 2GiB + 512MiB
long = timeout &gt; 1m
message = "giving up after {timeout}"
</code></pre></div>
<p>Add or subtract two values of the same kind, multiply one by an <code>Int</code>,
and use <code>//</code> to divide by an <code>Int</code> or by another value of the same kind.
Dividing by the same kind gives an <code>Int</code>. <code>%</code> gives the remainder of one
value by another of the same kind. Values of the same kind can be
compared. Durations and sizes never mix with each other, with <code>Int</code> or
with <code>Float</code>, and an integer is never a duration, so write <code>0s</code> rather
than <code>0</code>. <code>/</code> is not available, because the result would not be a whole
number of the smallest unit.</p>
<p>A value is shown as its simplest literal: <code>retry_after</code> above is <code>30s</code>,
<code>budget</code> is <code>3m</code>, and <code>memory</code> is <code>2560MiB</code>.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="records-and-types">Records and Types</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>A record type describes the fields a value supplies. GCSL uses structural
types: two declarations with the same structure describe the same type,
and a record can be used where fewer of its fields are required.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--declaring-a-shape">Declaring a shape <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--declaring-a-shape" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Use <code>type</code> to give a structure a reusable name. An annotation checks a
value against that structure. Defaults supply omitted fields in a record
literal checked against the type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Install = {from: String, exclude: List&lt;String&gt; = []}
let item: Install = {from = "src"}
item.exclude
</code></pre></div>
<p>Defaults are constant expressions, not arbitrary function calls. They are
applied where a record literal has the expected type; they don't add
fields to an already constructed variable with a smaller type.</p>
<p>An annotation also determines what fields later code can see. Extra
fields in a record literal produce a warning when an expected type hides
them. A structural alias does not introduce a distinct runtime identity.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--generic-types">Generic types <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--generic-types" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A type can take type parameters, named in angle brackets after its name.
Each use names the types to put in their place:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Pair&lt;A, B&gt; = {first: A, second: B}
let entry: Pair&lt;String, Int&gt; = {first = "port"; second = 8080}
entry.second
</code></pre></div>
<p>A use must give as many type arguments as the type has parameters, and a
type without parameters takes none. Because types are structural,
<code>Pair&lt;String, Int&gt;</code> is the same type as <code>{first: String, second: Int}</code>. A
generic type can be built from another, as in
<code>type Named&lt;T&gt; = Pair&lt;String, T&gt;</code>. It can't refer to itself, and it can't
spread one of its parameters, because the parameter might not be a record.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--enums">Enums <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--enums" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>An extensible enum is a type whose variants any module can add. Declare
the enum, with an optional bound that every variant's payload must fit,
and then its variants, each with a payload type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Shape extensible&lt;{*: Int}&gt;
enum Shape::Square&lt;{side: Int}&gt;
enum Shape::Point
square = Shape::Square({side = 3})
</code></pre></div>
<p>A variant is also a value: <code>Shape::Square</code> on its own has the type
<code>Variant&lt;Shape, {side: Int}&gt;</code>. Calling it makes a value of the enum,
and you can pass it to a function as a key. <code>kind.matches(value)</code> tests
whether a value is of that variant, and <code>kind.payload(value)</code> takes out
its payload:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>enum Tool extensible
enum Tool::C&lt;{cc: String}&gt;
fn find&lt;T&gt;(kind: Variant&lt;Tool, T&gt;, tools: List&lt;Tool&gt;, fallback: T): T {
    for t in tools { if kind.matches(t) { return kind.payload(t) } }
    fallback
}
cc = find(Tool::C, [Tool::C({cc = "gcc"})], {cc = "cc"}).cc
</code></pre></div>
<p>Unlike records, enums are nominal: two enums are different types even
if they look alike. <code>value is Tool</code> tests whether a value is of the enum.
Values of an enum are compared with <code>==</code> only when the enum has a bound,
because an unbounded payload might hold a function. A variant is visible
in its own file, to the other files of its directory and the directories
below it, and to every file that imports its module.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--partial-records">Partial records <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--partial-records" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>Partial&lt;T&gt;</code> holds any of the fields of the record type <code>T</code>. Use it to
describe a record by only some of its fields, and <code>.matches()</code> to test
a whole record against it:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
let wanted: Partial&lt;Platform&gt; = {arch = "x86_64"}
fits = wanted.matches({arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"})
</code></pre></div>
<p>A field of a partial record can't be read by name, because it may be
absent.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--records-of-any-fields">Records of any fields <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--records-of-any-fields" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A rest-field, <code>*: T</code>, gives the type of every field that a record type
doesn't name. Use it when the field names are data, such as paths. A
<code>{*: String}</code> holds any fields, each a <code>String</code>. Named fields keep their
own types, so <code>{to: String, *: Int}</code> holds a <code>String</code> field <code>to</code>, and any
other fields are <code>Int</code>s:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let files: {*: String} = {"bin/*" = "bin/*"; "sbin/*" = "sbin/*"}
files
</code></pre></div>
<p>Write the record literal where the type is expected: in an annotation,
an argument or a definition's result. There, each field that the type
doesn't name is checked against the rest-field. A value whose own type
has no rest-field doesn't fit one, because a record keeps fields that its
static type hides, and they could be of any type.</p>
<p>Read a named field as usual. A field that only the rest-field covers
can't be read by name, and a record whose type has a rest-field can't be
spread. Typed equality compares every field, and <code>is</code> and <code>as</code> test each
field that the rest-field covers.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--combining-fields">Combining fields <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--combining-fields" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Dotted definitions construct nested records. Compatible dotted paths
merge, including across files in a workspace:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>server.host = "localhost"
server.port = 8080
server.tls.enabled = true
</code></pre></div>
<p>A whole-field definition such as <code>server = {...}</code> closes that field to
further dotted extensions. Define the individual dotted leaves when you
want several files to contribute to a shared structure.</p>
<p>A spread starts from another record's visible fields. Inside an ordinary
record block, put defaults first and explicit overrides afterward:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let defaults = {host = "localhost"; port = 80}
let config = {
    ...defaults
    port = 8080
}
config.port
</code></pre></div>
<p>A spread copies only fields visible through its operand's static type.
It cannot replace an earlier explicit field inside an ordinary block.
Top-level declarations are resolved across the whole input and take
precedence over a spread of the same name regardless of textual order.</p>
<h2 id="records-and-types--unions-and-narrowing">Unions and narrowing <a class="heading-anchor" href="#records-and-types--unions-and-narrowing" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A union, such as <code>Int | String</code>, allows more than one shape. Use <code>is</code> to
test a value and <code>as</code> for a checked conversion that fails at runtime if
the value doesn't fit. In <code>if name is Type</code>, the branches see narrowed
copies of the named value. This does not perform general flow analysis of
later statements or dotted field paths.</p>
<p>Typed equality <code>==</code> compares operands of the same static type. Total
equality <code>===</code> compares complete runtime records, including fields hidden by
their shared static type. Both require the same static operand type. The
language's
restrictions on unions, functions, and futures are described in
<code>gcsl(5)</code> and <a href="#inference-limits">Inference Limits</a>.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="functions-and-effects">Functions and Effects</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>A function's signature says what it accepts, what it returns, and whether
calling it can perform effects. This makes executable configuration
reviewable: a pure calculation cannot silently run a process or read a
file.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--choosing-a-function-form">Choosing a function form <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--choosing-a-function-form" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Use <code>fn</code> for a private function and <code>def</code> for a function exposed as a
field. Both forms declare parameter and result types. A function
expression can also be assigned to a variable or passed to another
function.</p>
<p>The marker before the result type selects the effect:</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table><thead><tr><th>Marker</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>:</code></td><td>Pure computation; no effects or awaiting</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-&gt;</code></td><td>Impure computation; effects and awaiting are allowed</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>-&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>Impure computation memoized by its arguments</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<p>This private pure function accepts a named argument and a default:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn scale(value: Int, factor: Int = 2): Int { value * factor }
scale(value = 21)
</code></pre></div>
<p>Positional and named arguments are evaluated in source order. A trailing
block supplies the last parameter, which is useful for functions that
accept a configuration record.</p>
<p>A default can be any expression. It can use the parameters before it and
anything visible where the function is written:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let suffix = ".o"
fn object(source: String, output: String = "{source}{suffix}"): String {
    output
}
main = object("main.c")
lib = object("lib.c", output = "lib.a")
</code></pre></div>
<p>A default is evaluated each time a call leaves its parameter out, after
the arguments that are written and before the function's body runs. It
has the function's effect, so a pure function's default cannot call an
impure function. A default cannot use its own parameter or a later one.
Only a call by the function's own name uses its defaults. A call through
a copy, such as <code>g</code> after <code>let g = object</code>, or through a parameter must
supply every argument.</p>
<p>After a named argument, a name on its own is shorthand for passing the
variable of that name to the parameter of that name. These two calls are
the same:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn build(source: String, output: String, compiler: String): String {
    "{compiler} {source} -o {output}"
}
let compiler = "cc"
short = build("main.c", output = "main.o", compiler)
long = build("main.c", output = "main.o", compiler = compiler)
</code></pre></div>
<p>Before any named argument, a name on its own is an ordinary positional
argument.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--generic-functions">Generic functions <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--generic-functions" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A function can work on values of any type. Name its type parameters in
angle brackets after its name, and use them as types in its signature and
body:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn first&lt;T&gt;(items: List&lt;T&gt;, fallback: T): T {
    if len(items) &gt; 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }
}
port = first([8080, 9090], 80)
name = first([], "none")
</code></pre></div>
<p>Each call works out the type parameters from its arguments: here <code>T</code> is
<code>Int</code> in the first call and <code>String</code> in the second. Inside the function, a
value of type <code>T</code> can be passed on, returned, or put in a list or record,
but nothing else, because <code>T</code> could be anything. A call where nothing binds
a type parameter, such as one that only returns a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, is an error.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--methods">Methods <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--methods" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A method is a function you call on a value with a dot. Write its
receiver in parentheses before its name:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn (n: Int) twice(): Int { n * 2 }
fn (items: List&lt;T&gt;) first_or&lt;T&gt;(fallback: T): T {
    if len(items) &gt; 0 { items[0] } else { fallback }
}
doubled = 21.twice()
port = [8080].first_or(80)
</code></pre></div>
<p>The value before the dot is the receiver. GCSL chooses the method by
the receiver's type, so methods with the same name can serve different
types. When more than one fits, the one for the more specific type is
used: a method for <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code> is chosen over one for <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> when
the receiver is a <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code>. A method's other parameters, defaults and
effects work as a function's do.</p>
<p>A method has no name of its own: <code>twice</code> alone is not a variable, and
<code>x.twice</code> without parentheses reads a field. A record field and a method
with the same name are an error when both could apply.</p>
<p>Methods are declared at the top level of a file. A <code>fn</code> method is
visible only in its own file. A <code>def</code> method is also visible to the
other files of its directory and the directories below it, and to every
file that imports its module, whatever the form of the import. In the
REPL, methods are visible to later lines.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--calling-for-effects">Calling for effects <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--calling-for-effects" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A call to an impure or memoized function can stand alone as a statement,
outside a pure function, to run it for its effects. Its value is
discarded, whatever it is, and anything it defers is awaited first, so no
effect is lost:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn build(source: String) -&gt; String {
    sh("make -C {source}")
    sh("make -C {source} install")
    = source
}
</code></pre></div>
<p>Any other value standing alone is an error, because discarding it would
do nothing: a pure expression has no effects to keep. Make it the block's
result with <code>= ...</code>, bind it with <code>let</code>, or remove it.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--captures-and-recursion">Captures and recursion <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--captures-and-recursion" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Closures hold copies of the values they capture. Rebinding a variable
later doesn't change an existing closure. An inner function cannot rebind
an outer function's variable.</p>
<p>Top-level named functions are available regardless of textual order and
can refer to each other within the file. Workspace files still need an
acyclic dependency order: functions in two files cannot make those files
need each other. Put mutually dependent definitions together or extract
their shared dependencies.</p>
<h2 id="functions-and-effects--memoization">Memoization <a class="heading-anchor" href="#functions-and-effects--memoization" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A memoized function reuses a successful result for the same arguments in
the same execution context. The cache belongs to the closure, so two
closures created from one function expression have separate entries.
Memoization also accounts for supported ambient inputs such as a caller
path when the function reads it.</p>
<p>This is not a persistent build cache. External changes to files or a
command's environment do not invalidate a stored result. Use <code>-&gt;&gt;</code> only
when reusing the first result within that run is correct. Cached keys and
results consume memory for the lifetime of their execution context.</p>
<p>Read <a href="#deferred-work">Deferred Work</a> before using futures in function
signatures; a bare type's present-or-future meaning depends on its
position and the function's effect.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="attributes">Attributes</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>An attribute goes before a function and says how to run it, for example
whether a build step may be cached or how long it may take. GCSL checks
every attribute. Most are read by the tool that runs the program, such as
a build system, and GCSL itself acts on its own two, <code>retry</code> and
<code>timeout</code>.</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}

#[hermetic]
fn compile(source: String) -&gt; String { "compiled {source}" }

#[hermetic(false)] #[timeout(10m)]
fn deploy(target: String) -&gt; String { "deployed {target}" }

result = deploy(compile("main.c"))
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id="attributes--declaring-an-attribute">Declaring an attribute <a class="heading-anchor" href="#attributes--declaring-an-attribute" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>An attribute's arguments form a record, so you declare an attribute as a
record type marked <code>#[attribute]</code>. Each field is an argument, and a field
with a default is an argument you can leave out:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type device = {name: String, count: Int = 1}

#[device(name = "kvm")]
fn boot() -&gt; String { "booted" }

booted = boot()
</code></pre></div>
<p>Arguments must be constants: literals, or lists and records of them. A
field may be an <code>Int</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>Bool</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Duration</code> or <code>Size</code>, or
a list or record of those. An attribute is declared, imported and named in
the same way as any other type, so a tool usually provides its attributes
in a module that you import.</p>
<h2 id="attributes--using-an-attribute">Using an attribute <a class="heading-anchor" href="#attributes--using-an-attribute" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Write <code>#[NAME]</code>, or <code>#[NAME(ARGUMENTS)]</code>, on the line before a function: a
<code>fn</code>, a <code>def</code>, or a definition made with a definition type. An attribute
with one field can take its argument by position, as in <code>#[timeout(10m)]</code>;
one with more fields takes its arguments by name. A misspelt attribute is
an error, so a mistake isn't silently ignored.</p>
<p>GCSL provides two attributes of its own, <code>retry</code> and <code>timeout</code>:</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table><thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Argument</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>#[retry(times)]</code></td><td><code>times: Int</code></td><td>If the call fails, run it again, up to this many more times.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>#[timeout(limit)]</code></td><td><code>limit: Duration</code></td><td>Fail the call if it runs for longer than this.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<p>A retried call runs its whole body again with the same arguments, so any
effects it has happen again: use <code>retry</code> only where that's safe. Retrying a
pure function can't change its result, so GCSL warns about it. When a call
times out, a program it's running is stopped. A function's own timeout
counts as a failure that its <code>retry</code> can retry, and each attempt gets the
full time. A timeout of an enclosing call is never retried by a call inside
it.</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[retry(2)] #[timeout(30s)]
fn fetch(url: String) -&gt; String { exec(["echo", "fetched {url}"]).stdout }

page = fetch("https://example.com")
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id="attributes--attributes-on-a-definition-type">Attributes on a definition type <a class="heading-anchor" href="#attributes--attributes-on-a-definition-type" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Attributes on a definition type apply to every definition made with it.
A definition's own attribute of the same name replaces the definition
type's:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>#[attribute] type hermetic = {on: Bool = true}

#[def_type] #[hermetic]
def check(name: String, f: fn(): Bool): fn(): Bool { f }

def check fast() { true }
#[hermetic(false)] def check online() { true }

results = [(check fast)()(), (check online)()()]
</code></pre></div>
<p>Here <code>fast</code> is hermetic and <code>online</code> is not.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="context">Context</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>Some values matter deep inside a program without every function in
between caring about them: the platform you are building for, for
example, or the toolchains available. A <em>context</em> is a named value of
that kind. You declare it once, read it with <code>^</code>, and set it for a call
and everything that call does with <code>with</code>:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>context arch: String = "x86_64"

fn compiler(): String { "gcc-{^arch}" }
fn build(): String { "built with {compiler()}" }

native = build()
cross = build() with { arch = "aarch64" }
</code></pre></div>
<p><code>build</code> never mentions <code>arch</code>, yet <code>cross</code> is built with <code>gcc-aarch64</code>:
the <code>with</code> reaches every call made inside it.</p>
<h2 id="context--declaring-a-context">Declaring a context <a class="heading-anchor" href="#context--declaring-a-context" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>context NAME: TYPE = DEFAULT</code> declares a context and its default, at the
top level of a file. Every file of the workspace can read it without an
import, so a name is declared only once. A context can leave out its
default, <code>context toolchains: List&lt;Toolchain&gt;</code>; reading it then needs a
<code>with</code> that sets it, and reading it without one is an error.</p>
<p>A default is an expression, and it is evaluated where the context is
read, in the context of the read. So one context can default to another,
and follow it:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
context host_platform: Platform = {arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"}
context platform: Platform = ^host_platform

target = ^platform.arch with { host_platform = {arch = "aarch64"; os = "linux"} }
</code></pre></div>
<p>A default must be pure: it can't call an impure function. To use a value
that needs one, compute it first and bind it with <code>with</code>.</p>
<h2 id="context--setting-a-context-with-with">Setting a context with <code>with</code> <a class="heading-anchor" href="#context--setting-a-context-with-with" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>EXPR with { NAME = VALUE }</code> evaluates <code>EXPR</code> with the context <code>NAME</code>
set to <code>VALUE</code>. It applies to the call or field access just before it,
so <code>a + f() with {...}</code> sets the context for <code>f()</code> alone. It must be on
the line where the expression ends. To set a context for several
statements, use the block form, <code>with { NAME = VALUE } { ... }</code>.</p>
<p>A dotted name sets one field of a record context and keeps the others:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}
context platform: Platform = {arch = "x86_64"; os = "linux"}

fn triple(): String { "{^platform.arch}-{^platform.os}" }
riscv = triple() with { platform.arch = "riscv64" }
</code></pre></div>
<p><code>with { arch }</code> is short for <code>with { arch = arch }</code>, setting the context
from a variable of the same name.</p>
<h2 id="context--reading-a-context-in-a-parameter">Reading a context in a parameter <a class="heading-anchor" href="#context--reading-a-context-in-a-parameter" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A parameter written <code>^NAME</code> takes its default from the context of that
name, so a caller can still pass a value explicitly. <code>fn f(^arch)</code> is the
same as <code>fn f(arch: String = ^arch)</code>, with the type taken from the
context's declaration:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>context arch: String = "x86_64"
fn compiler(^arch): String { "gcc-{arch}" }

usual = compiler()
explicit = compiler(arch = "mips")
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id="context--context-and-deferred-work">Context and deferred work <a class="heading-anchor" href="#context--context-and-deferred-work" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A <code>lazy</code> keeps the context it was made in. Wherever it is later
awaited, it reads the values that were set where it was written. A
top-level field is computed when its file runs, so it reads the default
values.</p>
<p>Memoised functions stay correct: a memoised call's result is reused only
for a call made in a context that gives every context the call read the
same value.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="failure">Failure</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>A program fails when it hits a runtime error: a <code>panic</code>, an index out of
range, a program it runs exiting with an error, or a failed <code>assert</code>.
Normally that ends the run. Two constructs work with failure directly:
<code>assert</code>, which fails on purpose when something doesn't hold, and
<code>recover</code>, which catches a failure and continues.</p>
<h2 id="failure--asserting">Asserting <a class="heading-anchor" href="#failure--asserting" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>assert COND</code> fails the run if the condition is false. The error shows
the condition as you wrote it, and for a comparison it labels each side
with its value, so a failed check explains itself:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="text"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>error: assertion failed: `output == "hello"`
 --&gt; app.gcsl:2:8
  |
2 | assert output == "hello"
  |        ------    ------- this is `"hello"`
  |        |
  |        this is `"hullo"`
</code></pre></div>
<p>Give your own message after a comma, <code>assert ok, "the server did not start"</code>. Each side of a comparison is evaluated only once, so an
assertion about a program's output doesn't run the program twice.</p>
<h2 id="failure--recovering">Recovering <a class="heading-anchor" href="#failure--recovering" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>recover { BODY } else { FALLBACK }</code> evaluates the body, and if it fails,
evaluates the fallback instead. Name the failure to read its message:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>fn half(n: Int): Int {
    assert n % 2 == 0, "{n} is odd"
    n // 2
}

even = recover { half(10) } else { 0 }
odd = recover { half(7) } else { -1 }
message = recover { "half is {half(7)}" } else (failure) { failure.message }
</code></pre></div>
<p>The failure is a record, <code>{message: String}</code>. The two blocks join as an
<code>if</code>'s branches do, so they normally have one type.</p>
<p><code>recover</code> catches what <code>#[retry]</code> would retry, and nothing else. A bug in
GCSL itself is never caught. Neither is a timeout of a call that encloses
the <code>recover</code>: that timeout belongs to the enclosing call, which ends as
if the <code>recover</code> weren't there. A <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or <code>return</code> inside
the body is not a failure, and leaves through it as usual. Anything the
body did before it failed stays done.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="deferred-work">Deferred Work</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>A future represents a calculation whose result is needed later. <code>lazy</code>
creates that calculation without running it. Awaiting it evaluates the
body and remembers the successful result. Futures are deferred work, not
concurrent tasks.</p>
<p>This program creates one future and then reads its result:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let answer = lazy 21 * 2
await answer
</code></pre></div>
<h2 id="deferred-work--where-work-happens">Where work happens <a class="heading-anchor" href="#deferred-work--where-work-happens" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A <code>lazy</code> body is an impure context, even when a pure function creates it.
That lets a pure function plan effects without performing them. A pure
function cannot await a future. Resolve the value in an impure caller
before passing it into a pure calculation that needs the value itself.</p>
<p>The checker inserts implicit awaits at uses that need a value's contents,
such as arithmetic or a required present argument. It applies the same
purity restriction to explicit and implicit awaits. A call to a pure
function never secretly starts deferred work inside that function.</p>
<h2 id="deferred-work--writing-contracts">Writing contracts <a class="heading-anchor" href="#deferred-work--writing-contracts" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Use explicit wrappers when the distinction is part of an interface:</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Contract</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><code>Present&lt;Int&gt;</code></td><td>A present integer, equivalent to <code>Int</code> in an ordinary annotation</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Future&lt;Int&gt;</code></td><td>Deferred work yielding an integer</td></tr>
<tr><td>`Int</td><td>Future<Int>`</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<p>A present value does not satisfy an explicitly required <code>Future&lt;T&gt;</code>.
Nested future layers collapse: awaiting a future resolves it fully.
Awaiting an already present value is a silent no-op.</p>
<p>Bare types in impure parameters accept present or deferred values,
including bare positions inside records and lists. Pure parameters infer
whether their bodies need the value's contents. Impure and memoized
results infer their present-or-future shape from the body. Explicit
wrappers keep the contract you wrote.</p>
<h2 id="deferred-work--avoiding-surprising-inference">Avoiding surprising inference <a class="heading-anchor" href="#deferred-work--avoiding-surprising-inference" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A pure function with result <code>Int</code> requires a present result, even if it
only returns its argument. It does not preserve a future's shape
polymorphically. Recursive result inference also retains conservative
parts of the declared signature. Use <code>Present&lt;T&gt;</code> or an explicit future
contract where an API needs a stable distinction, and consult
<a href="#inference-limits">Inference Limits</a> when an inferred type is wider
than expected.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="lists-and-control-flow">Lists and Control Flow</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>Lists hold ordered values of one element type. GCSL's loops are
expressions, so a loop can collect results into a list or run only for its
effects. This avoids a separate collection-building language.</p>
<h2 id="lists-and-control-flow--lists-and-ranges">Lists and ranges <a class="heading-anchor" href="#lists-and-control-flow--lists-and-ranges" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code> and <code>Int[]</code> name the same type. Indexes start at zero; a
negative or out-of-range index is a runtime error. A spread inserts
another list's elements. An empty list needs an expected element type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let empty: List&lt;Int&gt; = []
let values = [...empty, 3, 8, 5]
values[1]
</code></pre></div>
<p><code>0..3</code> produces <code>[0, 1, 2]</code>; <code>0..=3</code> includes <code>3</code>. Bounds are integers and
ranges run upward. A range used directly as a loop's iterable is counted
without first allocating the list of all its integers.</p>
<h2 id="lists-and-control-flow--collecting-and-repeating">Collecting and repeating <a class="heading-anchor" href="#lists-and-control-flow--collecting-and-repeating" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A loop used as a value collects each iteration's result. A guard skips
iterations that don't match:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let doubled = for n in [1, 2, 3] if n &gt; 1 { n * 2 }
doubled
</code></pre></div>
<p>A loop used as a statement discards its results. Use <code>for condition</code> for
repetition while a condition holds, <code>for { ... }</code> without a condition,
or <code>do { ... } for condition</code> to test after the body. A header can contain
initialization and an update:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let values = for let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i := i + 1 { i * 2 }
values
</code></pre></div>
<p>A plain header variable can be rebound by the header. Write <code>let mut</code> if
the body must also update it.</p>
<h2 id="lists-and-control-flow--leaving-a-computation">Leaving a computation <a class="heading-anchor" href="#lists-and-control-flow--leaving-a-computation" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>break</code> ends the enclosing loop and <code>continue</code> skips to its next
iteration. Either can supply a value to the collecting loop before
leaving that iteration. An unlabeled <code>return</code> returns from the enclosing
function or <code>lazy</code> body.</p>
<p>Labels identify an outer loop or a block. A labeled <code>return</code> leaves that
target with one value; it does not append to a loop's accumulated list.
Control flow cannot jump out through a function boundary. See <code>gcsl(5)</code>
for label syntax and the distinction between collected and returned
values.</p>

  </section>
  <section class="print-article">
    <header>
      <h2 id="inference-limits">Inference Limits</h2>
      <p class="print-crumbs">GCSL / The Language</p>
    </header>
    <p>GCSL infers local values and selected present-or-future positions. It does
not infer every relationship between types. Explicit annotations help you
state an intended interface when a program reaches one of these limits.</p>
<p>The following boundaries affect ordinary programs:</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table><thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>What to write</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>An empty list has no independent element type</td><td>Give it an annotation or an expected <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> type</td></tr>
<tr><td>A pure function does not preserve arbitrary future shapes</td><td>Write the present or future contract explicitly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recursive impure results can retain a deferred possibility</td><td>Use <code>Present&lt;T&gt;</code> when the result must be present</td></tr>
<tr><td>General type parameters and intersections are unsupported</td><td>Write concrete structural types</td></tr>
<tr><td>There is no <code>None</code> value or optional-type shorthand</td><td>Represent alternatives using supported union members</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narrowing recognizes a plain name tested directly by <code>if</code></td><td>Bind a field to a name before testing it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narrowing does not establish facts after an early return</td><td>Keep uses inside the narrowed branch</td></tr>
<tr><td>A narrowed mutable name is a branch-local copy</td><td>Don't try to update the original through that copy</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>len</code> and <code>show</code> use special call rules</td><td>Call them directly or through supported aliases, rather than storing them as ordinary function values</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
<p>Expected types reach record fields, call arguments, and other supported
literal contexts. An unrelated sibling expression is not a general
constraint-solving mechanism. For example, annotate an empty list before
combining it with independently inferred values.</p>
<p>Warnings do not prevent execution. A union inferred from unlike branch
results can produce a warning while the program remains valid. Use
<code>gcsl check</code> to inspect the full set of errors and warnings, and
<code>gcsl run --dump-types FILE</code> when the inferred shape is unclear.</p>
<p>These are supported-language boundaries, not promises about a future
inference engine. The checker implementation and its regression tests
are the source for the behavior described here.</p>

  </section>
</div>

</main>

